{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfeRyTHAsQRiDfgNGFWFv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote a refinement of `motif_stack`, and the self-check printed `OK`. The single change gives each participant their own alternation preference, which targets the strongest critique.\n\n**Choice.** `motif_stack` is the closest loser to the incumbent `local_representativeness`, 47 nats behind (2.2× dse). The critique's strongest discrepancy, which survives FDR, is that people differ far more in how much they prefer alternation than any single-population model produces (observed SD 0.16 vs 0.054, z = 17).\n\n**Change.** Everyone keeps the shared four-motif stack automaton score, exactly as in `motif_stack`. Added on top is a per-person weight on the difference in alternation rate between the two sequences. That weight is drawn from a population distribution with 400 slots, so the next experiment's design can predict new participants from it.\n\n**Check result.** It passed every gate the check runs: imports, data contract, finite log-probability, a short MCMC fit, and finite ELPD-LOO. The short fit's ELPD-LOO was −1153.6, against the incumbent's −1380.4. Treat that as a rough hint only:\n- It came from one chain with 100 draws.\n- The check flagged PSIS-LOO as unreliable: 6.1% of trials have Pareto k > 0.5, above the 1% tolerance. That is likely a side effect of the 400 per-person parameters.\n- The check could not verify convergence: R-hat is unavailable from a single chain.\n- Novelty against the live models was not checked.\n\nThe short fit took 36 seconds for 200 iterations, so the full admission fit should finish well inside the 30-minute limit.\n\nThe model is named `motif_stack_person_alternation`. Files are in `.../experiment1/model_loop/iter_0/candidate_5/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usag